 "text": "Hey, how are you doing right now? It' likely worn rivets that are letting tension slip. You can re tension it, but it' worth comparing it to a fresh chain to see how much wear it's suffered. Go for it, just start fresh fire first. Little means small and easy to fix, not dead. A chain that skips is neither. With the shifters pulled, of course, and never under load. Turn it one way until the highest gears stop jumping, then switch sides and turn the other way until the low gears stop jumping. Then it' time for a chain saw and some old sprockets. Chains are cheap, sprocket wear is another story.",
